<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wülzburg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1588">1588</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Wülzburg is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> fortress on a hill above Weißenburg in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, built from 1588 by the margraves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg-ansbach">Brandenburg-Ansbach</a> on the site of a Benedictine abbey. Its five bastions make it one of the best-preserved Renaissance fortresses in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-gaulle">Charles de Gaulle</a> was held there as a prisoner of war.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wulzburg.jpg" alt="Wülzburg" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Myratz at German Wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Luftbild_Wuelzburg.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
